WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.694
I was invited along to a party in Notting Hill, which in those days was not the

00:00:05.694 --> 00:00:08.000
smart Notting Hill you now know.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.032
Invited to a party by a friend of mine who was trying to make a living as an

00:00:14.032 --> 00:00:18.000
independent filmmaker, a man called Morris Hatton.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.000
And suddenly my wife and I turned up. This would be in early 1965.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
I'd just become deputy editor of the magazine News Society.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.500
And my friend Morris said, here's a chap you should meet.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:41.500
And so Cedric appeared looming through the grey smoke of a party of that kind.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.500
And we went out into the garden so we could hear each other speak.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:53.500
And Cedric said, well, yes, News Society, he said, News Society.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.960
excellent magazine, not enough jokes, you should meet my friend Bannam.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.660
That was the architecture and design critic Peter Rayner Bannam,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.660
whom I'd never met.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.200
And I thought, well, that's an idea.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.960
So next day I rang Bannam,

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.600
who became our architecture and design correspondent,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.720
and he wrote some wonderful articles,

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.280
often of a kind that might have been inspired by Cedric in a way.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:32.665
he would take some perfectly ordinary object, I mean one of the best known cases was

00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:33.000
the

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.875
manufacture of an ice cream cornet, or the growth of the new kind of crisp, not the

00:01:40.875 --> 00:01:41.160
old

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.969
fashioned crisp but a very special crisp with special flavors, and analyze it with

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:46.380
all the

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:52.587
techniques of an art historian, as if he were looking at a Giotto or Guido Renna or

00:01:52.587 --> 00:01:53.260
whatever.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:00.323
And so I was very grateful to Cedric for that, and we continued to see each other.

00:02:00.323 --> 00:02:00.540
And

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:09.217
the thing about that joke was that I think that Cedric realised that jokes and wit

00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:10.300
were one way

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.991
to put your point across. So many people think that to put your point across you've

00:02:14.991 --> 00:02:15.500
got to be

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:23.480
deeply serious and boring all the time, which Sadiq wasn't. From that meeting we

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:28.160
saw each other a lot. New South also thought that there were things to be

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:34.580
said for jokes and for provoking people and there was a lot of discussion at

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:40.640
that time as indeed there is now. What exactly the role of Parliament was, was

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.900
it properly representing the majority of the country, was it buried in a little

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:55.755
whole of its own that wasn't very interesting. And one of the phrases of the day was

00:02:55.755 --> 00:02:56.660
pop-up.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:02.315
It is again now isn't it, we talk about pop-up restaurants. Anyway it was in there

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:02.660
then.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:08.742
I thought well let's have a pop-up parliament. Let's pull down this old Gothic

00:03:08.742 --> 00:03:09.600
thing, over

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.985
and done with, not entirely seriously of course, and replace it with something high

00:03:15.985 --> 00:03:16.600
tech and

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:23.043
all that. So I rang Cedric up and said, would he like to write that? Now I didn't

00:03:23.043 --> 00:03:23.600
realise

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.829
what I was getting myself into because Cedric was not a writer actually, not in the

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:31.180
novel

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:40.196
sense of the word. He was a spark off of ideas, brilliant actually. But like many

00:03:40.196 --> 00:03:41.420
architects,

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:47.416
it's not unusual to find architects can't write. It doesn't somehow fit with their

00:03:47.416 --> 00:03:48.220
discipline.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:56.413
You get a series of statements, but not a persuasive article. And this article we

00:03:56.413 --> 00:03:57.020
talked

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:03.806
about for quite a long time, and it didn't come, and it didn't come, and it didn't

00:04:03.806 --> 00:04:04.220
come.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:09.732
And Cedric was then in his, not in the office that he became famous for in Alfred

00:04:09.732 --> 00:04:10.140
Place,

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:16.025
but it was in George Street behind the Wallace Collection. So I wandered along

00:04:16.025 --> 00:04:16.780
there, and

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:22.946
I said, Cedric, we're going to have to talk this article through. And eventually the

00:04:22.946 --> 00:04:23.460
article

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.277
did appear as by Cedric Price, but if you persevere to the end there's little

00:04:28.277 --> 00:04:28.840
footnotes

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:35.934
saying this article is the result of many conversations between the editor and Mr

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:36.460
Price.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:43.516
Well as you might imagine, it was, like everybody in the 60s, it was important to

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:44.300
clear the

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:50.857
space so you pulled the existing building down, you didn't try to adapt it in any

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:51.100
way

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:59.798
And you created something which very much depended on electronic and technological

00:04:59.798 --> 00:05:00.540
instant

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:06.220
communication between voters and members of parliament.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:10.447
So you didn't have the members of parliament here in this lovely box and the voters

00:05:10.447 --> 00:05:10.600
out

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:16.260
here whom you consulted once every five years.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.540
The idea was to create a continuous dialogue.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.798
dialogue. Would have been hell actually probably. That was the idea. And actually in

00:05:24.798 --> 00:05:25.320
a funny

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:31.515
sort of way with the arrival of social media, something like that is actually

00:05:31.515 --> 00:05:32.320
happening.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:39.518
I'm sure members of parliament feel for better or worse really. They're drawn much

00:05:39.518 --> 00:05:39.920
more.

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:46.599
So it was a beginning shot at that time. And I don't think either Cedric or I

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:47.500
thought this

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:54.259
with a project that anyone would ever build or demolish. But it was a way of pushing

00:05:54.259 --> 00:05:54.500
the

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:03.500
argument forward through built form rather than just simply stating what you felt.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:10.663
And so it was sometime after that that Cedric approached me with the idea for

00:06:10.663 --> 00:06:11.500
Pottery's

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:19.560
Thinkbelt because given the experience of the Pop-Up Parliament where he

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.740
realized that I could get blood out of a stone editorially, in other words

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:29.400
something that a general public might read as opposed to an architectural

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:38.620
public. Potteries Thinkbelt was to be cited in the landscape where Cedric was

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:45.257
was a child, in fact. North of Birmingham you have this area called the Potteries,

00:06:45.257 --> 00:06:45.500
for

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:52.023
the obvious reason that that is where the one element in the Industrial Revolution

00:06:52.023 --> 00:06:52.500
began.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:59.500
It was an area of manufacture of ceramics at one end, or simply old-fashioned plates

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:06.920
and dishes at the other. At the top of the ladder was a Wedgwood family, who created

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:10.920
who created a very special kind of English ceramics.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.920
And at the bottom were people who were making just mugs to put your tea in.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.920
And it was a big business.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:24.920
But like a lot of businesses in the post-war period in Britain,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.920
it had gone into decline.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:30.920
In fact, it had been going into decline for quite a long time,

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.920
partly through international competition

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:43.382
and partly through just bad practice by the people running the factories who were

00:07:43.382 --> 00:07:43.800
very

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.680
reluctant to invest new money in it.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.600
So it was a rather desperate area, and in some ways if you were to go there now it's

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.160
still a rather desperate area.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.880
There was nothing beautiful about it.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:59.680
No money had been put into buildings.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:08.275
And Cedric's idea was to take this area, which he knew well, take the example of an

00:08:08.275 --> 00:08:09.000
attempt

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:14.185
to build a new university there at a place called Keele, which had never been very

00:08:14.185 --> 00:08:14.880
successful.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.140
It was always a rather timid launch.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:20.680
It was always looking over its shoulder at other people.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:27.142
And he wanted to create something which would build on the existing rail network to

00:08:27.142 --> 00:08:27.920
an extent,

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:34.445
because the place was festooned with small railway lines because of the industrial

00:08:34.445 --> 00:08:35.320
background.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.640
It was quite easy to get from one place to the other.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:46.280
And he envisaged starting a university of 20,000 students immediately, who would go

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:56.600
from place to place, rather than building an aesthetically wonderful university.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.452
would have this thing which was more closely integrated with the community, you

00:09:00.452 --> 00:09:00.940
would have

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:07.600
the community making much more use of the university. You wanted to have more people

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.604
drawn in. Other people at the time were thinking already of using mass media,

00:09:13.604 --> 00:09:14.540
particularly

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:19.655
the newly popular television, which it's hard to think had a time when it was not

00:09:19.655 --> 00:09:20.160
popular,

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.039
but anyway it was only becoming popular. And not very far away they created

00:09:25.039 --> 00:09:26.080
something called

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:32.608
the Open University, which was based on the mass media and based in the new town of

00:09:32.608 --> 00:09:33.080
Milton

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:39.710
Keynes. And in a strange way, the Open University was building on Cedric's idea, but

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:40.420
not using

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:46.160
anything of the built form at all. They were just using the content.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:52.609
I'm just writing a long article about Nonplan because of the four people who wrote

00:09:52.609 --> 00:09:53.160
it, I'm

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:58.160
I'm the only one still alive. I'm an exhibit from the past.

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:09.708
And the two moving spirits on this attempt to again jolt people into thinking about

00:10:09.708 --> 00:10:14.160
what is the purpose of planning.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:23.803
planning the two moving space with myself and a geographer called Peter Hall, who

00:10:23.803 --> 00:10:24.160
has

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.370
just himself died and I'm just in the middle of writing a sort of tribute piece for,

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:29.680
which

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:34.160
is basically about how he and I collaborated on non-plan.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:41.549
Though he became a planner and great figure of a quite different sort, but at that

00:10:41.549 --> 00:10:42.000
time,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.500
like the rest of us in the mid-60s.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.560
We thought you could play around with anything really.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:54.380
Anything went really, was the point, intellectually as well.

00:10:54.380 --> 00:11:01.540
And Peter wrote for us a very great deal

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:08.740
on matters of urban change, often on planning.

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:15.730
But also he had a wide grasp of so many subjects, particularly based on his

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:17.780
experience of America.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:26.815
And both of us were dismayed and made angry by how modernist planning and

00:11:26.815 --> 00:11:28.300
architecture

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:33.789
had worked out on the ground, not what it was like in a book by Le Corbusier, but

00:11:33.789 --> 00:11:34.060
what

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.780
it was like when you built it.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:44.568
And particularly of course the claims that both planners and architects at the time

00:11:44.568 --> 00:11:45.140
usually

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:52.895
made for the way they were able to reshape the society that they were in, which

00:11:52.895 --> 00:11:53.680
involved

00:11:53.680 --> 00:12:03.200
a great deal of demolition of existing property, which could have been reused.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:08.744
I lived in the East End of London which was being destroyed by planning schemes,

00:12:08.744 --> 00:12:09.160
almost

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:15.660
more than it had been destroyed by the Luftwaffe.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:21.970
So Peter and I were having a bite to eat and we thought to ourselves, well, would it

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:22.120
be

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:24.680
any worse if there was no planning at all?

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.320
After all, the cities we've got weren't planned.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:34.080
They just grew up over centuries and planners had this program.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:37.760
Architects, particularly modernist architects, had this program.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:41.560
For example, the tower blocks were an excellent idea for families.

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.560
It was one of the more obvious things.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:50.200
And it was just an unhappy situation.

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:54.440
And once again, rather than purely say that,

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:56.640
we thought we should try to demonstrate it.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.240
And as we were talking, the idea of something called non-plan,

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:06.700
which now I've entered the language a bit, cropped up.

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.680
In other words, we should have a special issue of the magazine

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:16.560
which would propose the idea that if we had no planning,

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.280
if we perhaps listened to what people wanted for their houses

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:23.480
as opposed to imposing something on them, perhaps it would be okay.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:32.829
And so we agreed to do that. We also thought we should have some fellow writers on

00:13:32.829 --> 00:13:33.340
this.

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:39.403
And the two writers we went to immediately, there was no question about it, was

00:13:39.403 --> 00:13:39.940
Cedric,

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:46.960
who was the most iconoclastic architect we knew, and Banham, the man he'd originally

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:55.761
recommended to us and both of them immediately said yes. The structure of non-plan

00:13:55.761 --> 00:13:56.620
was that

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:02.039
there would be a couple of general articles, an introduction and a conclusion. And

00:14:02.039 --> 00:14:02.700
in between

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:08.736
those, in order to infuriate people, because that was part of the point, there would

00:14:08.736 --> 00:14:08.880
be

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:15.380
three case studies of bits of England that might be improved, we would argue,

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:16.140
destroyed

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:22.954
other people might argue, by being treated as areas that could be subjected to

00:14:22.954 --> 00:14:23.740
non-plan.

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:31.361
It took quite a long time to write because I became editor of the magazine in early

00:14:31.361 --> 00:14:31.820
1968.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:40.048
1968 was a year of explosions throughout the world on the student campuses and there

00:14:40.048 --> 00:14:40.440
were

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:45.060
lots of other things to write about apart from writing about non-plan.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:49.400
But we did eventually write it. I wrote both the introduction, Peter and I wrote

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:49.780
most of

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:58.269
the conclusion. Bannum decided to write about East Anglia as the set piece example.

00:14:58.269 --> 00:14:58.780
Peter

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:06.376
Hall wrote about East Midlands. Cedric himself wrote about the area of the New

00:15:06.376 --> 00:15:07.100
Forest on

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:12.641
South Coast, which again, as in the case of the Thinkbelt, he knew well that

00:15:12.641 --> 00:15:13.260
apparently

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:18.700
he'd moved there to escape bombing during the war and had spent quite a bit of time

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:26.285
with his grandfather, I think. Everybody wrote bits and put it together and we

00:15:26.285 --> 00:15:27.160
published

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:35.098
it in the Special History. And it's quite interesting, it provoked a mixture of

00:15:35.098 --> 00:15:35.600
anger

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:40.078
and sort of stunned silence. People couldn't believe that we were making this

00:15:40.078 --> 00:15:40.660
suggestion

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:49.200
because planning was one of those sacred things.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.000
If something wasn't planned, it wasn't quite real, really.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:58.640
I mean, there's an excellent book by a historian called Tony Yud,

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.060
which is just called Post-War,

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:04.860
and he takes the whole of Western Europe

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:08.560
and tries to understand whether,

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:14.529
in partial imitation of the Soviet system or for some other reason, everything was

00:16:14.529 --> 00:16:14.820
much

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:20.666
better if it was planned rather than if it was just allowed to happen, which was a

00:16:20.666 --> 00:16:20.880
new

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:28.480
idea really, and of course in many places has now dwindled. But I would say that in

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:34.303
England at any rate in the 1960s the idea that everything had to be planned. You

00:16:34.303 --> 00:16:34.740
needed

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:41.280
to get the facts and the figures, then you plan something. It was at its height. And

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:47.780
a lot of people made a living out of this kind of thing, either as professionals or

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:57.821
as teachers or whatever. And people ask themselves, well, is this anarchy, letting

00:16:57.821 --> 00:16:59.780
anything happen,

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:05.493
Or is it kind of a covert new form of capitalism where you're just providing a new

00:17:05.493 --> 00:17:06.120
market so

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:08.160
people can make their money out of it?

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:10.620
A bit of both, really.

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:14.140
The philosopher Wittgenstein had a theory.

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:18.340
He didn't mind what he ate, provided it was always the same.

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:22.420
And in terms of appearance, that was Cedric.

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:28.072
He was always dressed the same, with his striped black-white shirt, his loose

00:17:28.072 --> 00:17:29.320
collar, his black

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:37.067
knitted tie is cigar and if you were talking to him at the office it was a glass of

00:17:37.067 --> 00:17:37.720
brandy.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:46.244
That was Cedric. An amazingly recognisable figure. The office he was mostly known

00:17:46.244 --> 00:17:46.560
for

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:51.857
was an office in a side street called Alfred Place, not far from the main buildings

00:17:51.857 --> 00:17:52.240
of the

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:57.908
University of London, not very far either from the post office tower which Peter

00:17:57.908 --> 00:17:58.900
Rainer Branham

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:03.940
once said it was the only building that had been built since the Second World War in

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:04.300
London

00:18:04.300 --> 00:18:11.841
that could deserve a space in the same skyline as St Paul's Cathedral. So, you know,

00:18:11.841 --> 00:18:12.200
very

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:17.575
much Cedric territory. The office was above a shop of some sort. I can't remember

00:18:17.575 --> 00:18:17.840
what

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:24.180
the shop was. So you went up some stairs, got the top, and there was... when you got

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:29.578
the top of the stairs there was some unfortunate character who was some kind of

00:18:29.578 --> 00:18:30.740
assistant sitting

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:34.657
there whose main job as far as I could see was to make sure that the radio was

00:18:34.657 --> 00:18:35.460
always turned to

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:44.323
Radio 4 and he would probably have talked to one or two of these assistants, Steve

00:18:44.323 --> 00:18:45.620
Mullin, Will

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:51.471
Alsop. There was always some such person there but Cedric wouldn't be there. Cedric

00:18:51.471 --> 00:18:52.740
would be one stage

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:59.386
further up in the building in the white room, which was a room painted white. It was

00:18:59.386 --> 00:18:59.940
a quite

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:08.394
large room. It was the full width of the building, I think. And there would be piles

00:19:08.394 --> 00:19:09.300
of papers

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:14.468
and bits of drawings and all the rest of it. You'd be offered a glass of brandy,

00:19:14.468 --> 00:19:14.920
because

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:21.093
there was always a glass of brandy, and you would talk for the next two hours, which

00:19:21.093 --> 00:19:21.240
is

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:25.240
which is why you couldn't gather that often because you had other things to do.

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:32.240
But it was always very enjoyable and from time to time somebody else would bob up,

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:36.240
the General Secretary of the trade union movement or whoever.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:42.240
He was a very generous host when he gave a party.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:47.240
And he came round here to our house a few times.

00:19:47.240 --> 00:19:51.685
times. The first time he came he deeply embarrassed my wife because he'd gone

00:19:51.685 --> 00:19:52.840
upstairs to wash his

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:57.479
hands and he came back down and said he'd never seen such a beautiful object. I

00:19:57.479 --> 00:19:58.360
thought what on

00:19:58.360 --> 00:20:04.294
earth has he seen? Sally had been trying to save soap by moulding the old bits of

00:20:04.294 --> 00:20:05.320
soap together,

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:11.662
you see, sort of some blue, some brown, some yellow, some white in a beautiful ball.

00:20:11.662 --> 00:20:12.040
So he

00:20:12.040 --> 00:20:17.320
They brought this all down to tell us that was the future.

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:23.880
Curiously, there's a Palestinian activist and philosopher

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:25.700
called Edward Said.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:29.280
He's one of the founders of the Divine Orchestra

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:33.440
between Israelis and Palestinians,

00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:37.780
and very much the New York intellectual.

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:41.720
But he told a friend of mine that the height of his career

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:48.101
was when he went to a dinner party in Manhattan somewhere, and his fellow guest was

00:20:48.101 --> 00:20:49.100
Sid Charisse,

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:57.342
the dancer for An American in Paris. Well, when Cedric was in Las Vegas for some

00:20:57.342 --> 00:20:57.960
event,

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:04.481
I don't think he was a gambler, but he would believe very strongly in the ethos of

00:21:04.481 --> 00:21:04.720
Las

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:10.720
Vegas, as you get in the Venturi book on learning from Las Vegas. That was his

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:11.720
scene. And he

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:18.917
was in a restaurant sitting there having his supper with a friend. And he noticed

00:21:18.917 --> 00:21:19.400
across

00:21:19.400 --> 00:21:25.771
the dance floor in the restaurant there was Cid Charisse sitting there. Well, Cid

00:21:25.771 --> 00:21:26.400
Charisse

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:31.351
never wanted to lose his opportunities of this kind, so he went across to Cid

00:21:31.351 --> 00:21:31.820
Charisse

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:34.260
and said, can I have a dance?

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:37.880
And so the height of that trip, and perhaps of more than that

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:40.180
for Cedric, was that that was the night

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:42.100
I danced with Cedris.

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:43.840
It's something that I never managed

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:47.320
to pin down in talking to him, the extent to which he would

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:52.640
have liked, really in his heart, to have built more.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:54.780
He always maintained, at least in talking to me,

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:56.740
that yes, he did want to build more,

00:21:56.740 --> 00:21:58.620
but there was this problem with the bureaucrats

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:00.740
and there was that problem.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:07.740
But I'm not sure. I think for him building was a kind of dialogue.

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:12.740
And of course he did such beautiful drawings in particular.

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:19.740
Whatever I may half jokingly say about him as a writer, he was brilliant.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:24.740
As a speaker, his lectures were wonderful. I went to a few of them.

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:33.540
And he could just take an audience and turn them around in his hand.

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:45.175
And teach people not through long, elaborate arguments, but through jokes, through

00:22:45.175 --> 00:22:47.020
observations,

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:53.060
through asking questions to which there was no obvious answer.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:23:01.060
And I think it's an intellectual power that he has.

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:08.654
I can't see, speaking as a non-architect, I can't see much evidence that

00:23:08.654 --> 00:23:09.920
architecture

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:15.060
has gone in a way that he would approve of.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:16.320
I can't see that at all.
